# Venu Pacific — Phase 1 MVP package set
# One package name per line. Verified against the trixie repos on 2026-07-20.

# Desktop — XFCE (task-xfce-desktop pulls in xfce4, lightdm, and friends).
# task-xfce-desktop pulls trixie's stock xfdesktop4 4.20.1-1, which has a
# confirmed wallpaper-rendering bug — see
# config/hooks/normal/0120-venu-pacific-xfdesktop-fix.hook.chroot, which
# replaces it with 4.20.2 after packages are installed.
task-xfce-desktop

# wget needed by the xfdesktop4 fix-up hook above to fetch the two .deb
# files directly (not added as an ongoing apt source — see the hook for why).
wget

# Boot splash — themed via config/hooks/normal/0100-venu-pacific-plymouth.hook.chroot
# (our own theme ships via includes.chroot below; plymouth-themes isn't
# needed and would just add unused theme files to the image)
plymouth

# Icon theme — chose an existing, maintained theme instead of building a
# custom icon set from scratch; that's a much larger effort than Phase 1
# branding calls for. Papirus is actively maintained, GTK/XFCE-friendly, and
# light enough for older hardware.
papirus-icon-theme

# Browser
firefox-esr

# Office / creative
libreoffice
gimp

# Offline knowledge (Kiwix — offline Wikipedia/content reader)
kiwix

# kiwix-search (kiwix-tools) and zimdump (zim-tools) let the AI assistant
# search and read the bundled Vikidia ZIM programmatically -- the `kiwix`
# package above is only the GUI app (kiwix-desktop), it has no CLI. Verified
# directly against the real bundled vikidia.zim before adding these:
# kiwix-search returns matching article titles, zimdump show --url=<title>
# returns the article's raw HTML, which venu-pacific-assistant strips to
# plain text via python3-bs4 before handing it to the model.
kiwix-tools
zim-tools
python3-bs4

# pdftotext (poppler-utils, <1MB installed) lets the AI assistant extract
# the text layer from PDF documents for its read_file tool -- school
# worksheets and government forms are exactly the documents this audience
# will ask about, and without this a PDF read fed the model raw binary
# garbage instead of failing cleanly.
poppler-utils

# Installer (present in the live image so the target machine can install to
# disk). The `calamares` package is only the framework — with nothing else,
# there's no /etc/calamares/settings.conf or module sequence, so it can't
# actually install anything. calamares-settings-debian provides Debian's
# real settings/branding/module config; confirmed by inspecting a built
# image's squashfs (usr/bin/calamares present, /etc/calamares absent
# entirely) before this was added.
calamares
calamares-settings-debian

# Software center — Synaptic. Chose it over GNOME Software: no PackageKit
# or GNOME-stack dependency, fits the low-spec-hardware constraint, and
# it's a mature, direct apt front-end (install/remove/search/upgrade) that
# needs no custom UI work for Phase 1.
synaptic

# Update manager (notify-send is the notification backend for
# venu-pacific-notify-updates, see config/config/includes.chroot)
libnotify-bin

# Error dialogs for shell launchers (venu-pacific-kolibri) -- a
# Terminal=false .desktop script that fails silently reads as "clicking
# the icon does nothing"; zenity gives those scripts a real dialog.
# Verified NOT already on the image (not in the built chroot's dpkg
# status), unlike the two below.
zenity

# The AI assistant hard-requires these at import/runtime, so they must be
# EXPLICIT dependencies, not inherited accidents. Both were already
# landing on the image, but only via Recommends chains: gir1.2-wnck-3.0
# through task-xfce-desktop -> Recommends orca (the screen reader!) ->
# Depends gir1.2-wnck-3.0, and cups-client (lpstat, cancel) through
# task-desktop -> Recommends cups. Either chain snapping in a future
# slimming pass (dropping orca is exactly the kind of cut a low-spec
# image invites) would take the assistant down with it: Wnck is a hard
# gi.require_version at startup, lpstat/cancel are the whole printer
# troubleshooting flow.
gir1.2-wnck-3.0
cups-client

# Welcome app (welcome-app/venu-pacific-welcome) — GTK3 via PyGObject,
# not GTK4: XFCE's own native apps here are still GTK3 (confirmed:
# xfdesktop 4.20 reports "Built with GTK+ 3.24"), so this matches rather
# than adding a second GTK stack for one small first-run window.
python3-gi
gir1.2-gtk-3.0

# VM guest integration — spice-vdagent enables host<->guest clipboard
# sharing and display resizing under SPICE (virt-manager); qemu-guest-agent
# lets the host query/manage the guest (graceful shutdown, IP reporting).
# Both are inert on real hardware: they only activate when the matching
# virtio devices are detected, so they cost nothing outside a VM.
spice-vdagent
qemu-guest-agent

# NOTE: VS Code / VSCodium from the roadmap is deferred — neither ships in
# Debian's own repos, so including it means adding a third-party apt archive
# (config/archives/) and trusting its signing key. Revisit once we decide
# whether that's worth the added dependency, or ship python3-idle instead.

# Venu Pacific's own packages, built by scripts/build-debs.sh into
# config/config/packages.chroot/ — live-build turns that directory into a
# local apt repository, so naming the metapackage here installs the whole
# set with proper dependency resolution rather than a bare `dpkg -i`.
#
# This is also what makes updates work after installation: everything here
# is dpkg-owned and carries the archive keyring, so `apt update` on a user's
# machine sees venupacific.org's own archive alongside Debian's, and
# `apt upgrade` can carry these packages forward without a new 4.7GB image.
venu-pacific-desktop
